WEBVTT

00:00:00.162 --> 00:00:01.939
Last video ended with this question.

00:00:02.563 --> 00:00:04.531
Tear one page at random off this desk

00:00:04.551 --> 00:00:07.042
calendar, and every page has the same chance.

00:00:07.685 --> 00:00:09.715
But is every date, from the first to

00:00:09.736 --> 00:00:11.242
the thirty-first, equally likely?

00:00:11.807 --> 00:00:12.272
Make a guess.

00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:15.083
Let's flip through a whole year and count

00:00:15.104 --> 00:00:16.147
the pages for each date.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:18.967
January has every date from one to thirty-one.

00:00:19.432 --> 00:00:20.625
February stops at twenty-eight.

00:00:21.252 --> 00:00:22.433
By the end of the year, the dates

00:00:22.635 --> 00:00:25.218
one to twenty-eight have twelve pages each, the

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.104
twenty-ninth and thirtieth have eleven, and the thirty-first

00:00:28.245 --> 00:00:29.045
has only seven.

00:00:29.749 --> 00:00:32.344
Every page is equally likely, but the date

00:00:32.547 --> 00:00:32.747
isn't.

00:00:33.389 --> 00:00:35.917
So when is a distribution uniform, and how

00:00:35.938 --> 00:00:36.358
do we check?

00:00:37.021 --> 00:00:40.692
A discrete random variable is uniformly distributed if

00:00:40.732 --> 00:00:43.019
it has finitely many values, all with the

00:00:43.059 --> 00:00:43.881
same probability.

00:00:44.765 --> 00:00:47.397
Uniform just means even, like a flat row

00:00:47.479 --> 00:00:48.061
of bars.

00:00:48.725 --> 00:00:51.153
And the shared probability isn't a free choice.

00:00:51.837 --> 00:00:54.591
Say there are n values, each with probability

00:00:54.612 --> 00:00:54.651
c.

00:00:55.679 --> 00:00:58.207
They must add to one, so n times

00:00:58.308 --> 00:01:01.097
c is one, and c is one over

00:01:01.319 --> 00:01:01.358
n.

00:01:02.262 --> 00:01:03.725
The page itself is uniform:

00:01:04.228 --> 00:01:07.115
three hundred and sixty-five pages, each with probability

00:01:07.517 --> 00:01:09.141
one over three hundred and sixty-five.

00:01:10.063 --> 00:01:12.810
A fair die has six values, so each

00:01:13.011 --> 00:01:13.813
is one sixth.

00:01:14.535 --> 00:01:16.779
A random digit from zero to nine has

00:01:16.921 --> 00:01:19.345
ten values, so each is one tenth.

00:01:20.047 --> 00:01:22.451
So when something is chosen with equal chances

00:01:22.532 --> 00:01:25.518
from a finite list, a uniform model fits.

00:01:26.529 --> 00:01:28.584
So why isn't the date uniform?

00:01:29.252 --> 00:01:30.205
Look behind each bar.

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:32.558
The first of the month sits on twelve

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:35.009
equally likely pages, one in every month.

00:01:35.552 --> 00:01:38.698
The thirty-first sits on just seven, because February,

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.365
April, June, September and November are too short.

00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:43.969
So P of the first is twelve over

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:45.793
three hundred and sixty-five, and P of the

00:01:45.834 --> 00:01:48.197
thirty-first is only seven over three hundred and

00:01:48.218 --> 00:01:48.738
sixty-five.

00:01:49.544 --> 00:01:50.132
Here's the test.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:53.167
Uniform is a property of the random variable,

00:01:53.409 --> 00:01:54.271
not of the equipment.

00:01:55.134 --> 00:01:57.239
Ask: does every value come from the same

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.164
number of equally likely outcomes?

00:01:59.666 --> 00:02:01.342
For the date, the answer is no.

00:02:02.082 --> 00:02:04.698
The classic case is two fair dice, added.

00:02:05.482 --> 00:02:07.950
Make one die white and one red, so

00:02:08.192 --> 00:02:10.539
three then four is a different outcome from

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:11.523
four then three.

00:02:12.446 --> 00:02:15.799
That gives thirty-six outcomes, and with independent fair

00:02:15.839 --> 00:02:17.646
dice they're all equally likely.

00:02:18.509 --> 00:02:21.056
If you only list the twenty-one unordered pairs,

00:02:21.437 --> 00:02:24.325
they aren't equally likely, because a double happens

00:02:24.525 --> 00:02:26.531
one way and a mixed pair two ways.

00:02:27.455 --> 00:02:29.625
A sum of twelve needs six and six,

00:02:29.988 --> 00:02:30.851
just one outcome.

00:02:31.536 --> 00:02:33.405
A sum of seven runs along this whole

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:35.174
diagonal, six outcomes.

00:02:35.717 --> 00:02:37.864
So seven is six times as likely as

00:02:37.905 --> 00:02:38.225
twelve.

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:41.417
The outcomes are uniform, but the sum is

00:02:41.498 --> 00:02:41.718
not.

00:02:42.515 --> 00:02:45.424
Once a variable really is uniform, every value

00:02:45.485 --> 00:02:48.474
carries one over n, so any probability is

00:02:48.536 --> 00:02:49.177
just a count.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.685
Count the values you want, and divide by

00:02:51.866 --> 00:02:51.905
n.

00:02:52.789 --> 00:02:55.073
For a random digit, P of X at

00:02:55.174 --> 00:02:58.480
least seven counts seven, eight and nine, so

00:02:58.521 --> 00:02:59.221
it's three tenths.

00:03:00.086 --> 00:03:01.130
Be careful counting n.

00:03:01.654 --> 00:03:04.021
Suppose X is uniform on the whole numbers

00:03:04.062 --> 00:03:05.285
from five to twenty.

00:03:05.888 --> 00:03:08.436
Twenty minus five is fifteen, but that misses

00:03:08.537 --> 00:03:08.636
one.

00:03:09.139 --> 00:03:11.487
Both ends count, so there are sixteen values.

00:03:12.189 --> 00:03:14.970
Then P of X greater than sixteen counts

00:03:15.031 --> 00:03:18.732
seventeen, eighteen, nineteen and twenty, which is four

00:03:18.773 --> 00:03:20.373
sixteenths, or one quarter.

00:03:21.064 --> 00:03:21.612
Two more checks.

00:03:22.521 --> 00:03:26.129
Say X is uniform on five, ten, fifteen,

00:03:26.269 --> 00:03:27.412
twenty and twenty-five.

00:03:28.214 --> 00:03:30.701
P of fifteen is one fifth, because there

00:03:30.721 --> 00:03:31.483
are five values.

00:03:32.185 --> 00:03:33.730
It has nothing to do with fifteen being

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:35.675
in the middle, or adding up the values.

00:03:36.277 --> 00:03:38.401
That kind of reasoning would make twenty-five more

00:03:38.461 --> 00:03:39.322
likely than five,

00:03:39.843 --> 00:03:41.967
and in a uniform model every value is

00:03:42.127 --> 00:03:42.769
equally likely.

00:03:43.732 --> 00:03:44.688
And you can work backwards.

00:03:45.293 --> 00:03:47.816
If every value has probability zero point zero

00:03:47.897 --> 00:03:50.841
four, then n is one over zero point

00:03:50.882 --> 00:03:52.924
zero four, which is twenty-five values.

00:03:53.725 --> 00:03:56.309
But zero point three can't be the probability

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:58.833
of each value, because one over zero point

00:03:58.875 --> 00:04:00.176
three isn't a whole number.

00:04:01.039 --> 00:04:03.092
A group of values could still have probability

00:04:03.133 --> 00:04:03.797
zero point three.

00:04:04.628 --> 00:04:05.530
One last condition:

00:04:06.213 --> 00:04:07.777
finitely many values.

00:04:08.661 --> 00:04:10.605
Watch what happens as the number of equal

00:04:10.666 --> 00:04:11.588
values grows.

00:04:12.473 --> 00:04:15.189
Six values, twelve, twenty-four, forty-eight.

00:04:15.753 --> 00:04:17.621
Each bar is one over n,

00:04:18.205 --> 00:04:19.972
so the bars get shorter and shorter.

00:04:20.716 --> 00:04:23.282
Now try infinitely many values, all with the

00:04:23.323 --> 00:04:24.044
same probability.

00:04:24.768 --> 00:04:27.523
If that probability is zero, the total is

00:04:27.584 --> 00:04:27.966
zero.

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:30.874
If it's anything bigger than zero, the total

00:04:30.955 --> 00:04:32.939
passes one once there are enough values.

00:04:33.803 --> 00:04:35.190
Either way it can't be one.

00:04:36.035 --> 00:04:39.688
That's why a discrete uniform distribution needs finitely

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:40.572
many values.

00:04:41.336 --> 00:04:43.805
Continuous ones come later, and they work differently.

00:04:44.388 --> 00:04:45.634
Here's a way to remember the test.

00:04:46.259 --> 00:04:48.269
Same count behind every bar.

00:04:49.113 --> 00:04:51.619
Look behind each value of X, and count

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:53.383
the equally likely outcomes there.

00:04:54.107 --> 00:04:56.199
This only works if those outcomes really are

00:04:56.361 --> 00:04:57.125
equally likely.

00:04:57.809 --> 00:04:59.113
If every count is the same,

00:04:59.737 --> 00:05:00.680
X is uniform.

00:05:01.363 --> 00:05:03.676
The calendar's dates fail this test, and so

00:05:03.697 --> 00:05:05.004
do the sums of two dice.

00:05:07.737 --> 00:05:08.201
Your turn.

00:05:09.368 --> 00:05:12.478
X is uniformly distributed on the even numbers

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:13.723
from two to twenty.

00:05:14.486 --> 00:05:17.109
Find P of X greater than fifteen.

00:05:17.896 --> 00:05:18.728
Pause here to answer.

00:05:29.381 --> 00:05:30.603
The values are two,

00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:31.505
four,

00:05:32.026 --> 00:05:34.851
six, up to twenty, which is ten values,

00:05:35.252 --> 00:05:37.356
so each has probability one tenth.

00:05:38.257 --> 00:05:42.141
Greater than fifteen means sixteen, eighteen or twenty,

00:05:42.681 --> 00:05:43.302
three values.

00:05:43.822 --> 00:05:46.419
So P of X greater than fifteen is

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:47.204
three tenths.

00:05:48.129 --> 00:05:49.455
Three twentieths is wrong.

00:05:49.939 --> 00:05:52.434
Twenty is the largest value, not the number

00:05:52.455 --> 00:05:52.938
of values.

00:05:54.522 --> 00:05:54.886
Last one.

00:05:55.471 --> 00:05:58.315
A fair spinner with eight equal sectors, numbered

00:05:58.495 --> 00:06:00.157
one to eight, is spun once.

00:06:00.959 --> 00:06:03.181
X is the remainder when the number is

00:06:03.221 --> 00:06:04.103
divided by three.

00:06:04.988 --> 00:06:06.603
Is X uniformly distributed?

00:06:07.489 --> 00:06:08.252
Pause here to answer.

00:06:20.236 --> 00:06:22.204
X can be zero, one or two.

00:06:22.707 --> 00:06:24.416
Count the sectors behind each value.

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:28.186
Remainder zero has two sectors, and remainders one

00:06:28.227 --> 00:06:29.790
and two have three sectors each.

00:06:30.593 --> 00:06:33.474
So the probabilities are two eighths, three eighths

00:06:33.716 --> 00:06:34.395
and three eighths,

00:06:34.897 --> 00:06:36.117
and X isn't uniform.

00:06:36.879 --> 00:06:38.986
Saying it's uniform because the spinner is fair

00:06:39.188 --> 00:06:39.669
is wrong.

00:06:40.132 --> 00:06:42.389
Fair sectors don't make a fair variable.

00:06:44.134 --> 00:06:47.336
So a distribution is uniform when every value

00:06:47.397 --> 00:06:50.179
has the same count of equally likely outcomes

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:50.700
behind it,

00:06:51.221 --> 00:06:53.963
and then each value has probability one over

00:06:54.184 --> 00:06:54.263
n.

00:06:55.266 --> 00:06:56.372
Next, a new question.

00:06:57.057 --> 00:06:58.325
Here's the fair die again.

00:06:59.049 --> 00:07:01.031
If these bars were weights on a beam,

00:07:01.552 --> 00:07:02.372
where would it balance?

00:07:05.235 --> 00:07:06.037
Here are the key notes.

00:07:06.681 --> 00:07:08.408
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:08.951
to copy them.
